{% extends 'shells/docs.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Common Pitfalls · php-via Docs{% endblock %}
{% block head %}
{{ parent() }}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{% verbatim %}{
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "The page renders but nothing reacts",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "The page has no via_head or no via_foot, so it never opens its SSE stream or never loads Datastar. php-via logs a warning for such a shell or route. Fix: write {{ via_head }} right after <meta charset> and {{ via_foot }} before </body> in a shell, {{ via_head() }} and {{ via_foot() }} in a Twig layout, or $c->viaHead() and $c->viaFoot() in a callable that returns a whole document."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "signal() throws after scope()",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "The primary scope set by scope() is no default for signals. After scope() set a shared scope, a signal without a scope throws a LogicException, because it would silently be private to the tab. Fix: pass the scope as the third argument, $c->signal(0, 'count', Scope::ROUTE), or leave it out on purpose for a value private to the tab before calling scope()."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "$c->broadcast() updates only my own tab",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Context::broadcast() broadcasts the context's primary scope, and the default primary scope is TAB, so it syncs that one tab. Fix: set a shared primary scope with $c->scope(...), or broadcast the scope you mean with $app->broadcast('room:lobby'). A scoped signal broadcasts its scope by itself when a write changes it."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Other tabs show stale values after a broadcast",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "When broadcast() fires, every tab in the scope renders its view again. A view that reads a TAB signal shows each tab its own copy, which the action changed for the tab that posted it only. Fix: keep shared state in a scoped signal, $c->signal(0, 'votes', Scope::ROUTE), which every tab reads, or in $app->globalState()."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Shared state kept in globalState and a signal at once",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Every context that declares a signal with the same name and scope shares one value, and a write broadcasts the scope. Mirroring it into globalState and calling broadcast() by hand keeps two sources of truth. Fix: declare $c->signal(0, 'count', Scope::GLOBAL) and call $count->increment() in the action."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Every tab of a route pattern shares one ROUTE scope",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Scope::ROUTE resolves to the route pattern, so /chat/lobby and /chat/kitchen both share the scope of /chat/{room}. Fix: use a custom scope per room, such as 'room:' . $room, for signals and broadcasts."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "A shared render shows one user's data to everyone",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "view(..., shareRender: true) renders each update once and sends the same HTML to every tab of the view. A view that reads a TAB signal, the session or a component renders the first tab's version for all of them. Fix: drop shareRender from views that differ per tab; keep it for views that are the same for everyone, such as a game board."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Two buttons that post the same action cancel each other",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Datastar cancels a request still in flight when any element sends another one with the same method and URL. A request cancelled before it reaches the server never runs its action. Fix: give each element a query of its own, @post('{{ send.url }}?from=button'), or pass {requestCancellation: 'disabled'} where every request counts."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "A dialog opened with showModal() closes on the next update",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "A morph sets each element's attributes to those of the server's markup, and every update and reconnect renders the view again, so the open attribute that showModal() set goes. Fix: render the dialog with data-preserve-attr=\"open\"."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "A checkbox set by a script does not update its signal",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Datastar 1.0.4 updates a signal bound to a checkbox or radio on the input event, not on change. Fix: dispatch an input event after setting checked, or bind with the __event.change modifier."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "A value the browser posts is ignored",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Scoped signals are server-owned unless declared clientWritable: true, withStrictTabSignals() makes TAB signals server-owned, and a value of another type than the signal's initial value is refused unless it converts without loss. Dev mode warns once per signal. Fix: declare the signal clientWritable: true, give it an initial value of the type the browser sends, or declare it with null to take any type."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "data-text uses the wrong signal id",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "A signal's browser id is not its name: TAB and component signals get an id that is unique per tab, and the exact form is not part of the API. Fix: write $signal->ref() or $signal->id() in PHP, and {{ count.ref }} or {{ count.id }} in Twig. Never write an id by hand."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Full-page patch overwrites component updates",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "A page view renders the whole page on every broadcast that reaches it, and on a reconnect or a revival. Component HTML rendered once in the page handler goes out with each of those frames and puts the page-load HTML back over the components. Fix: call the component callables inside the view callable, so each page render carries their current HTML."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "A component in a loop throws about its namespace",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "A component namespace is unique on its page and takes letters, digits, _ and - only, since it prefixes the component's signals and action URLs. Fix: build it from a key, $c->component(Item::class, 'item-' . $item->id)."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "The view shows the values from page load",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "An array passed to $c->view() is built once, when the page handler runs, so every later render shows the values it held then. Fix: pass the data as a callable, which runs on every render: $c->view('t.html.twig', fn (): array => [...])."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Variable is null inside a view closure",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "A PHP closure declared with function () captures only the variables in its use clause, and the page handler's use clause and the view's are separate. Fix: add the variable to both, or write the view as an arrow function, which captures outer variables by itself."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "PatchElementsNoTargetsFound on block updates",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "An update without a selector morphs the element whose id matches the root element of the patch. A block without a single root element that has an id leaves Datastar nothing to match. Fix: give the block one root element with a unique id."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "A block update replaces the whole page (duplicate id=\"content\")",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "The default shell wraps the view in an element with id=\"content\". A block whose root element also uses id=\"content\" replaces that wrapper. Fix: give the block's root element an id the shell does not use."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Timer keeps broadcasting when nobody is watching",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "$app->setInterval() runs for the life of the server and knows nothing about clients. Fix: skip the work when $app->countClients($scope) is 0, which counts the connected tabs a broadcast of the scope reaches on every worker."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Shell templates do not support Twig",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "A shell is plain HTML with placeholders that php-via replaces as strings, so Twig tags in it come out as text. Fix: move the layout into a Twig base template that renders the whole document with {{ via_head() }} and {{ via_foot() }}, and let page templates extend it."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "A template view throws that the app has no template engine",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "twig/twig does not come with php-via. Fix: run composer require twig/twig and set withTemplateDir(), or return HTML from a callable view."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "A Config change after new Via() throws",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "new Via($config) freezes the Config, so a later with* call throws a LogicException instead of being ignored or half applied. Fix: make every with* call before new Via(), or work on clone $config, which is not frozen."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Datastar stops working under a Content-Security-Policy",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Without data-nonce on the <html> element, Datastar runs data-* expressions through the Function constructor, which needs 'unsafe-eval', and a nonce policy blocks the scripts the server sends. Fix: set a nonce in the via.csp_nonce request attribute and allow the nonce alone. via_head and via_foot write it on their tags, and the default shell writes data-nonce on <html>; a custom shell needs <html{{ via_html_attrs }}> and a Twig layout <html{{ via_html_attrs() }}>."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Action returns HTTP 400 Bad Request",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "A 400 means the request carried no via_ctx, or one that names no context php-via holds or can rebuild. Common causes: the server restarted, the tab was away longer than the revival window, or a form posted with contentType: 'form' lacks a via_ctx field. Fix: reload the page, or add <input type=\"hidden\" name=\"via_ctx\" value=\"{{ contextId }}\"> to the form."
            }
        }
    ]
}{% endverbatim %}
</script>
{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article>
    <h1>Common Pitfalls</h1>
    <p>
        Bugs that are easy to hit and hard to diagnose. Each one describes what goes wrong, why it happens,
        and how to fix it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="connecting">Pages that do not connect</h2>

    <details open>
        <summary><strong>The page renders but nothing reacts</strong></summary>
        <p>
            The page loads, but clicks do nothing, <code>data-text</code> never changes and the browser opens
            no request to <code>/_sse</code>.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> The page has no <code>via_head</code>, which opens the SSE stream, or no
        <code>via_foot</code>, which loads Datastar. php-via logs a warning once for such a shell or route.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Write the bootstrap where it belongs:</p>
        {% code 'html' %}{% verbatim %}
<!-- shell.html -->
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    {{ via_head }}
    ...
</head>
<body>
    ...
    {{ via_foot }}
</body>
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
        <p>In a Twig layout write <code>{% verbatim %}{{ via_head() }}{% endverbatim %}</code> and
        <code>{% verbatim %}{{ via_foot() }}{% endverbatim %}</code>, and in a callable that returns a whole
        document <code>$c-&gt;viaHead()</code> and <code>$c-&gt;viaFoot()</code>. See
        <a href="/docs/twig#bootstrap">Templates → The page bootstrap</a>.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Datastar stops working under a Content-Security-Policy</strong></summary>
        <p>
            With a policy in place, the console shows <code>EvalError</code> or <code>GenerateExpression</code>
            errors, or scripts sent with <code>execScript()</code> never run.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> Without <code>data-nonce</code> on the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element, Datastar
        runs every <code>data-*</code> expression through the <code>Function</code> constructor, which needs
        <code>'unsafe-eval'</code>, and a nonce policy blocks the scripts the server sends.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Set a nonce in the <code>via.csp_nonce</code> request attribute and allow the
        nonce alone. <code>via_head</code> and <code>via_foot</code> write it on their tags, and the default shell
        writes <code>data-nonce</code> on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>. A custom shell needs
        {% verbatim %}<code>&lt;html{{ via_html_attrs }}&gt;</code>{% endverbatim %}, a Twig layout
        {% verbatim %}<code>&lt;html{{ via_html_attrs() }}&gt;</code>{% endverbatim %}. See
        <a href="/docs/twig#csp">Templates → Content-Security-Policy</a>.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Action returns HTTP 400 "Bad Request"</strong></summary>
        <p>
            A click posts to the action, the response is <code>400 Bad Request</code>, and nothing happens.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> Every action carries the <code>via_ctx</code> signal, which names the tab's
        context. A 400 means it is missing, or names a context php-via neither holds nor can rebuild. A tab that
        was away briefly is rebuilt (revival); these cases are not:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>The server restarted, and the records it rebuilds tabs from went with it.</li>
            <li>The tab was away longer than the revival window, 10 minutes by default (see
                <a href="/docs/api#config-with-context-timeouts"><code>withContextTimeouts()</code></a>).</li>
            <li>A form posted with Datastar's <code>contentType: 'form'</code> sends its fields instead of the
                signals, and has no <code>via_ctx</code> field.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> After a restart or a long absence, reload the page; the tab's next stream
        reconnect does so by itself. Give a form the field:</p>
        {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
<form enctype="multipart/form-data" data-on:submit__prevent="@post('{{ upload.url }}', {contentType: 'form'})">
    <input type="hidden" name="via_ctx" value="{{ contextId }}">
    <input type="file" name="avatar">
    <button>Upload</button>
</form>
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    </details>

    <h2 id="scopes">Scopes and broadcasts</h2>

    <details>
        <summary><strong><code>signal()</code> throws after <code>scope()</code></strong></summary>
        <p>
            <code>$c-&gt;signal(0, 'count')</code> throws a <code>LogicException</code> on a page that called
            <code>$c-&gt;scope(Scope::ROUTE)</code>.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> The primary scope from <code>scope()</code> is the target of
        <code>$c-&gt;broadcast()</code>, not a default for signals. A signal without a scope is private to the
        tab, and php-via throws rather than let a page that meant to share it silently keep it per tab.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Name the scope where the signal is declared:</p>
        {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->scope(Scope::ROUTE);
$count = $c->signal(0, 'count', Scope::ROUTE);   // shared by every tab on this route
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
        <p>A value private to the tab can still be declared before <code>scope()</code> is called.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong><code>$c-&gt;broadcast()</code> updates only my own tab</strong></summary>
        <p>
            An action changes shared state and calls <code>$c-&gt;broadcast()</code>, but only the tab that posted
            it renders again.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> <code>$c-&gt;broadcast()</code> broadcasts the context's primary scope, and the
        default primary scope is TAB, so it syncs that one tab. Scopes the tab joined with
        <code>addScope()</code> or a scoped signal are not its primary scope. Dev mode warns once when a tab
        that joined other scopes broadcasts this way.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Broadcast the scope you mean, or set a shared primary scope:</p>
        {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->broadcast('room:' . $room);   // every tab in the room

// or, for the whole page:
$c->scope(Scope::ROUTE);
// ...
$c->broadcast();                    // every tab on this route
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
        <p>A scoped signal needs neither: a write that changes it broadcasts its scope.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Other tabs show stale values after a broadcast</strong></summary>
        <p>
            You broadcast after an action, but the other tabs still show the old value.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> A broadcast renders every tab's view again. A view that reads a TAB signal
        shows each tab its own copy, and the action changed only the copy of the tab that posted it.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Keep the shared value where every tab reads the same one, in a scoped signal or
        in global state:</p>
        {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
// ❌ each tab reads its own copy
$votes = $c->signal(0, 'votes');

// ✅ every tab on the route reads one value, and a write broadcasts the route
$votes = $c->signal(0, 'votes', Scope::ROUTE);
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Shared state kept in <code>globalState</code> and a signal at once</strong></summary>
        <p>
            You track a shared value with <code>$app-&gt;globalState()</code> <em>and</em> a signal, keep both in
            step by hand and call <code>$app-&gt;broadcast()</code> yourself.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> A scoped signal already is shared state: every context that declares a signal
        with the same name and scope shares one value, and a write that changes it broadcasts the scope. A copy in
        <code>globalState</code> is a second source of truth to keep in step.</p>
        {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
// ❌ two sources of truth, kept in step by hand
$count = $c->signal($app->globalState('count', 0), 'count', Scope::GLOBAL);
$c->action(function () use ($app, $count): void {
    $app->setGlobalState('count', $app->globalState('count', 0) + 1);
    $count->setValue($app->globalState('count'));
    $app->broadcast(Scope::GLOBAL);
}, 'increment');

// ✅ the scoped signal is the only source
$count = $c->signal(0, 'count', Scope::GLOBAL);
$c->action(fn () => $count->increment(), 'increment');   // atomic across workers, broadcasts
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
        <p><strong>When is <code>globalState</code> right?</strong> For server-side values that no
        <code>data-text</code> or <code>data-bind</code> shows, such as a status a view reads, or data shared
        between routes without a common scope.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Every tab of a route pattern shares one ROUTE scope</strong></summary>
        <p>
            On <code>/chat/{room}</code>, a message posted in <code>/chat/lobby</code> shows up in
            <code>/chat/kitchen</code> too.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> <code>Scope::ROUTE</code> resolves to the route pattern,
        <code>Scope::routeScope('/chat/{room}')</code>, the same for every URL it matches.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Use a custom scope per room:</p>
        {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->page('/chat/{room}', function (Context $c, string $room): void {
    $messages = $c->signal([], 'messages', 'room:' . $room);
    // ...
});
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>A shared render shows one user's data to everyone</strong></summary>
        <p>
            With <code>view(..., shareRender: true)</code>, every tab shows the name or the cart of one visitor.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> A shared render renders each update once and sends the same HTML to every tab
        of the view. A view that reads a TAB signal, the session, per-user data or a component renders the
        first tab's version for all of them.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Leave <code>shareRender</code> out of views that differ per tab. Keep it for
        views that are the same for everyone, such as a game board, and move what differs into a separate view
        or into signals. See <a href="/docs/views">Views</a>.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Timer keeps broadcasting when nobody is watching</strong></summary>
        <p>
            A <code>$app-&gt;setInterval()</code> timer broadcasts to a route every second, also when nobody has
            it open.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> The timer runs for the life of the server and knows nothing about clients.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Skip the work when <code>countClients()</code> finds nobody. It counts the
        connected tabs a broadcast of the scope reaches, on every worker:</p>
        {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->setInterval(function () use ($app): void {
    if ($app->countClients(Scope::routeScope('/ticker')) === 0) {
        return;
    }
    $app->broadcast(Scope::routeScope('/ticker'));
}, 1000);
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    </details>

    <h2 id="browser">In the browser</h2>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Two buttons that post the same action cancel each other</strong></summary>
        <p>
            Pressing Enter in a field and clicking Send at once runs the action only once, or a quick second
            click drops the first.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> Datastar cancels a request still in flight when any element sends another one
        with the same method and URL. A request cancelled before it reaches the server never runs its action.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Tell the requests apart with a query, or turn cancellation off where every
        request counts:</p>
        {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
<input data-bind="{{ message.id }}" data-on:keydown="evt.key === 'Enter' && @post('{{ send.url }}?from=enter')">
<button data-on:click="@post('{{ send.url }}?from=button')">Send</button>

<button data-on:click="@post('{{ paint.url }}', {requestCancellation: 'disabled'})">Paint</button>
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
        <p>See <a href="/docs/actions#cancellation">Actions → Cancellation</a>.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>A dialog opened with <code>showModal()</code> closes on the next update</strong></summary>
        <p>
            A confirmation dialog closes by itself as soon as a broadcast or a reconnect renders the view.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> A morph sets each element's attributes to those of the server's markup, and
        every update and every stream connect renders the view again. The <code>open</code> attribute that
        <code>showModal()</code> set is not in the server's markup.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> List it in <code>data-preserve-attr</code>, in the markup the server renders:</p>
        {% code 'html' %}{% verbatim %}
<dialog id="confirm" data-preserve-attr="open">...</dialog>
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
        <p>See <a href="/docs/views#preserve-attr">Views → Attributes the browser sets</a>.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>A checkbox set by a script does not update its signal</strong></summary>
        <p>
            A script ticks a checkbox and dispatches <code>change</code>, but the bound signal keeps its value.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> Datastar 1.0.4 updates a signal bound to a checkbox or a radio button on the
        <code>input</code> event.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Dispatch <code>input</code>, or bind with the <code>__event.change</code>
        modifier:</p>
        {% code 'js' %}{% verbatim %}
box.checked = true;
box.dispatchEvent(new Event('input', { bubbles: true }));
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>A value the browser posts is ignored</strong></summary>
        <p>
            The browser shows a new value in a bound input, but the action sees the old one, and the next sync
            puts the old value back.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> php-via writes a posted value into a signal only when the signal takes client
        values: TAB signals do unless <code>withStrictTabSignals()</code> is on, scoped signals only with
        <code>clientWritable: true</code>. The value must also have the type of the signal's initial value; a
        lossless form such as <code>'5'</code> for an int is converted, anything else is refused. Dev mode warns
        once per signal.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Declare the signal <code>clientWritable: true</code>, give it an initial value
        of the type the browser sends, or declare it with <code>null</code> to take any type:</p>
        {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$note  = $c->signal('', 'note', Scope::ROUTE, clientWritable: true);
$limit = $c->signal(10, 'limit');      // '25' from a text input arrives as 25
$pick  = $c->signal(null, 'pick');     // any type
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>data-text uses the wrong signal id</strong></summary>
        <p>
            You write <code>data-text="$votes"</code>, and the element never updates or shows nothing.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> A signal's browser id is not its name. TAB and component signals get an id
        that is unique per tab, and the exact form is not part of the API.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Let php-via write it: <code>ref()</code> for expressions, <code>id()</code> or
        <code>bind()</code> for bindings.</p>
        {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->view(fn (): string => "<span data-text=\"{$votes->ref()}\">{$votes->int()}</span>");
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
        {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
<span data-text="{{ votes.ref }}">{{ votes.int }}</span>
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    </details>

    <h2 id="views">Views and components</h2>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Full-page patch overwrites component updates</strong></summary>
        <p>
            You use <code>$c-&gt;component()</code>, but every broadcast to the page puts the components back to
            how they looked at page load.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> A page view renders the whole page on every broadcast that reaches it, and on a
        reconnect or a revival, and Datastar morphs the page with it. Component HTML rendered once in the
        handler, such as <code>'counter' =&gt; $counter()</code> in an array passed to <code>view()</code>, goes
        out with each of those frames.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Call the component callables inside the view callable, so each page render
        carries their current HTML:</p>
        {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->view(fn (): string => $c->render('page.html.twig', ['counter' => $counter()]));
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>A component in a loop throws about its namespace</strong></summary>
        <p>
            <code>$c-&gt;component(Item::class, 'item')</code> in a loop throws an
            <code>InvalidArgumentException</code> on the second pass.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> A namespace prefixes the component's signals and action URLs, so it is unique on
        its page and takes letters, digits, <code>_</code> and <code>-</code> only.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Build it from a key:</p>
        {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
foreach ($items as $item) {
    $rows[] = $c->component(Item::class, 'item-' . $item->id);
}
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>The view shows the values from page load</strong></summary>
        <p>
            Updates arrive, but the view keeps showing what it showed at page load.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> An array passed to <code>$c-&gt;view()</code> is built once, when the page
        handler runs. Every later render shows the values it held then.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Pass the data as a callable, which runs on every render:</p>
        {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->view('presence.html.twig', fn (): array => ['count' => $app->countClients(Scope::routeScope('/'))]);
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Variable is null inside a view closure</strong></summary>
        <p>
            A <code>$c-&gt;view()</code> closure fails with "Call to a member function on null" or an undefined
            variable.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> A closure declared with <code>function ()</code> captures only the variables in
        its <code>use</code> clause, and the page handler's clause and the view's are separate.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Add the variable to both, or write the view as an arrow function, which captures
        outer variables by itself:</p>
        {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
// ❌ $repo was never captured by the page handler
$app->page('/', function (Context $c): void {
    $c->view(function () use ($repo): string {
        return '<ul id="list">' . $repo->listItems() . '</ul>';
    });
});

// ✅
$app->page('/', function (Context $c) use ($repo): void {
    $c->view(fn (): string => '<ul id="list">' . $repo->listItems() . '</ul>');
});
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>PatchElementsNoTargetsFound on block updates</strong></summary>
        <p>
            You send only a Twig block on updates with <code>view(..., block: 'demo')</code>, the console shows
            <code>PatchElementsNoTargetsFound</code>, and nothing updates.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> An update without a selector morphs the element whose <code>id</code> matches
        the root element of the patch. A block without a single root element that has an <code>id</code>
        leaves Datastar nothing to match. Dev mode logs a warning once per route for such an update.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Give the block one root element with a unique <code>id</code>:</p>
        {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
{% block demo %}
<ul id="todo-list">
    {% for item in items %}
        <li>{{ item.text }}</li>
    {% endfor %}
</ul>
{% endblock %}
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>A block update replaces the whole page (duplicate <code>id="content"</code>)</strong></summary>
        <p>
            After the first update, the page's layout disappears and only the block is left.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> The default shell wraps the view in an element with <code>id="content"</code>.
        A block whose root element uses <code>id="content"</code> too replaces that wrapper.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Give the block's root element an id the shell does not use:</p>
        {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
{# ❌ collides with the shell's id="content" #}
{% block demo %}<div id="content">...</div>{% endblock %}

{# ✅ #}
{% block demo %}<div id="todo-list">...</div>{% endblock %}
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    </details>

    <h2 id="setup">Setup</h2>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>A template view throws that the app has no template engine</strong></summary>
        <p>
            <code>$c-&gt;view('page.html.twig')</code> throws a <code>LogicException</code>, or
            <code>new Via()</code> throws for <code>withTemplateDir()</code>.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> <code>twig/twig</code> does not come with php-via.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Install it, or return HTML from a callable view:</p>
        {% code 'bash' %}{% verbatim %}
composer require twig/twig
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>A Config change after <code>new Via()</code> throws</strong></summary>
        <p>
            <code>$config-&gt;withStaticDir(...)</code> after <code>new Via($config)</code> throws a
            <code>LogicException</code>.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> <code>new Via()</code> freezes the Config, so a later change throws instead of
        being ignored or half applied.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Make every <code>with*</code> call before <code>new Via()</code>. A test that
        needs a variant works on <code>clone $config</code>, which is not frozen.</p>
    </details>

    <details>
        <summary><strong>Shell templates do not support Twig</strong></summary>
        <p>
            Twig tags such as <code>{% verbatim %}{% extends %}{% endverbatim %}</code> or
            <code>{% verbatim %}{% block %}{% endverbatim %}</code> in a shell (the file passed to
            <code>withShellTemplate()</code> or <code>setShellTemplate()</code>) show up as text.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> A shell is plain HTML with placeholders that php-via replaces as strings. See
        <a href="/docs/twig#shell">Templates → Shells</a> for the placeholders.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Move the layout into a Twig base template that renders the whole document, and
        let page templates extend it. php-via then skips the shell:</p>
        {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
{# templates/shells/base.html.twig #}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    {{ via_head() }}
    <title>{% block title %}{% endblock %}</title>
</head>
<body>
    <main>{% block content %}{% endblock %}</main>
    {{ via_foot() }}
</body>
</html>

{# templates/pages/home.html.twig #}
{% extends 'shells/base.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Home{% endblock %}
{% block content %}<h1>Hello</h1>{% endblock %}
        {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    </details>

    <h2 id="debugging-tips">Debugging tips</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Open the <a href="/docs/dev-bar">Dev Bar</a>, on in dev mode: its SSE panel shows every patch
            that reached the page, and its Logs panel php-via's warnings.</li>
        <li>Add <code>&lt;pre data-json-signals&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;</code> to a template to see the browser's
            signals live. Remove it before you deploy.</li>
        <li>Run with <code>withLogLevel('debug')</code> to see every render, shared render hit and broadcast
            in the terminal.</li>
        <li>In dev mode a failed page shows the exception's class and message instead of
            "Internal Server Error".</li>
    </ul>

